cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing how
  actor-relative, multidimensional real resource and capital states K and
  expectation-forming structures P shape actors' actions and decisions A. P
  retains future outlooks, evaluations, preferences, credit, beliefs, and
  related structures that generate expectations and action directions; the
  term expectation stock refers to this expectation-generating role rather
  than to a stored point expectation. Expected real change E[Delta K] is
  represented separately as a flow formed from current K and P for a specified
  target and interval. K, P, A, Delta K, and Delta P are actor-relative and are
  not assumed to be scalar or probabilistic distributions. Actions can change
  both K and P, while non-agentic changes to K such as depreciation or natural
  loss are treated as an exception note. Interventions that affect P are first
  represented as actions by an intervening actor and then as induced Delta P,
  rather than as exogenous Delta P injections. In economic projections,
  interactions among actors' expected Delta K and actions can be mapped to
  existing microeconomic equilibrium processes. Aggregate expected real change
  after cancelling transfers within a defined exchange scope can be projected
  as expected total surplus without requiring scalarization of multidimensional
  K. Macroeconomic adjustment is mapped to existing real-nominal adjustment
  processes, while aggregate nominal indicators serve as proxies for the
  collective manifestation of P. These equilibrium and measurement mechanisms
  are not defined as VFT-specific universal laws.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
keywords:
  - value-field theory
  - multidimensional state
  - multi-actor system
  - resource dynamics
  - expectation stock
  - expectation flow
  - expected total surplus
  - microeconomic equilibrium
  - macroeconomic adjustment
  - nominal-real structure
  - action
  - measurement proxy
  - social dynamics
